             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase H2 of hybrid-start-a-flow.md. Owner-delegated Q2 decision: A, persist the prior revision context and modified attributes in the Datahike commit; see docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/q2-revisions-decision.md. The optimized read-through population scan alone costs 31-35 ms and 21.6-24.9 MB for about 714,000 datoms; lineage adds 22-41 ms and 41.9-58.9 MB with a missing ancestor on both measured heads. The live native prior context serializes to 5,439 bytes; its size grows with distinct revised attributes, not datoms or transactions. The bridge already has 58 evictions at the 512 MB budget, so reconstruction is not once per commit per process. A stored Datahike commit carries no revision map, so `datahike.connector/connect` and `datahike.versioning/attached-cache-context` open any commit the connection did not write as the conservative revision and every content-keyed cache misses on a fresh fork; Seon bridges it in memory (`seon.db/remember-revisions!`, kind :seon.db/commit-revisions, the chain walk in `seon.db/content-revision`), lost at eviction and restart. H2a, the Datahike fork: `datahike.writing/commit!` stores with each commit record its revisions, the writer's prior context advanced by the batch's modified attributes at this commit id (`datahike.writing/commit-revisions`, `datahike.db/advance-cache-context`), outside the commit identity's input; `datahike.connector/connect`, `datahike.versioning/attached-cache-context` and `deref-conn` read them (`datahike.writing/stored-revisions`); a record without them names its own commit as the conservative revision, as before, so no migration. The whole map is stored, not a parent delta needing an ancestry walk on load: default's busiest branch holds 161 entries, 5,555 bytes of raw Fressian (2026-10-11). H2b, Seon: `seon.db/remember-revisions!`, `seon.db/recorded-revisions`, the kind and the chain walk are deleted; `seon.db/content-revisions` is a lookup; `seon.cluster.registry/branch!` and `seon.cluster.registry/fork!` stop remembering. Proof: this test: fork from main, write one fork entity, release, reopen twice through fresh connections (`seon.cluster.store/open-branch!`, no process memory of the fork); `seon.db/content-revisions` over `seon.program/program-attributes` equals main's and the written attributes name the fork head (a raw store's value stays detached, Datahike's own test). Files: reference-code/datahike (fork bump), src/seon/db.clj, src/seon/cluster/registry.clj, test/seon/db_test.clj."
