 #:seon.task{:name "an-explicit-eid-outside-the-allocators-range-is-refused"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Datahike accepts an explicit numeric entity id that can never be an entity. Measured 2026-10-11 through `seon.db/transact!` on a disposable branch of default (retired) with [:db/add eid :db/ident k]: eid tx0+7 (536870919) accepted into the transaction-id range (it collides with a transaction entity); eid 7<<32|1 (30064771073, above emax) accepted, then invisible to `[?e :db/ident ?v]` scans and to the identity lookup by its value, so the next upsert of that value makes a silent duplicate. Same on a raw in-memory db (eid-ranges-opus.md, eid-ranges-sol.md). Fix at the producer, in the fork (reference-code/datahike): `datahike.db.transaction/transact-tx-data` refuses, in the entity position of :db/add, :db/retract and an entity map, a numeric eid above the current transaction id (a transaction not yet issued, or above `datahike.constants/emax`) as an ordinary transaction error (:error :entity-id/range); an issued transaction id stays accepted, since it names that transaction's metadata, which Datahike's own valid-time and tx-meta writes annotate, and `seon.db/transact!` returns it as its declared refusal with no allocator moved. An explicit eid BELOW tx0 that the allocator never issued stays accepted and observed, as `datahike.db.transaction/advance-max-eid` does today: under `seon.db/entities-born-on-two-branches-of-one-commit-have-different-ids` the store's counter advances to it as well, so no later allocation collides; refusing it would break Datahike's own tests, which transact small explicit eids on fresh dbs. Proof: this test (an unissued transaction id and one above emax refuse through `seon.db/transact!` and max-eid is unchanged; an unissued eid below tx0 is accepted and the next fresh entity allocates above it). Files: reference-code/datahike (fork bump), test/seon/db_test.clj."
