 #:seon.task{:name "entities-born-on-two-branches-of-one-commit-have-different-ids"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase P1a of hybrid-agent-world.md (owner, 2026-10-11: one global counter per store, incremented as entity ids are handed out, so data is unique from birth and the squash merge replays a new entity's datoms with its eid unchanged). Today `datahike.versioning/branch!` copies the head's max-eid and `datahike.db.transaction/next-eid` is (inc max-eid), so two forks of one commit allocate the same eids (Opus: 60192 vs 60204 later; Sol: both eid 1). The fork change (reference-code/datahike, about 60 lines): (1) `datahike.db.transaction/next-eid` takes the report and allocates one past both max-eid and :datahike.db.transaction/eid-floor on it, the last id the store's AtomicLong reserved when the writer began the transaction; the writer (`datahike.writing/with-store-eids`) then reserves the db-after's max-eid by compareAndSet from that floor and reruns the transaction above the new floor if another branch reserved meanwhile, so a refused or retried transaction spends no ids and a single-branch store numbers exactly as before; its five call sites (`datahike.db.transaction/entity-map->op-vec` twice, `datahike.db.transaction/transact-tx-data` twice, `datahike.db.transaction/transact-entities-directly` once) pass the report they already hold; (2) `datahike.writing/transact!` passes the floor through `datahike.core/with`'s new arity, so the plain `datahike.core/with` (speculation: Seon's d/with test members, `seon.db/with`) and a db with no store keep the local (inc max-eid) and spend no ids; (3) a per-store registry, one AtomicLong keyed by the store id `datahike.gc-guard` already uses, created at the store's first `datahike.connector/connect` in the process and removed by delete-database, seeded from 1 + the max :max-eid over the roster: `k/get` :branches, then per branch `k/get` of its head and `datahike.writing/resolve-head` (a compact head names its commit record), reading the record's :max-eid field, which `datahike.writing/stored-db?` requires of every record Datahike accepts as a head, so no index or datom is loaded: O(branches) small reads once per process; (4) an explicit eid below tx0 above the counter advances the counter as `datahike.db.transaction/advance-max-eid` advances max-eid (observed, never refused), and an eid at or above tx0 refuses (`seon.db/an-explicit-eid-outside-the-allocators-range-is-refused`); a counter reaching tx0 refuses loudly. `max-eid` keeps meaning the branch's observed high-water mark (`datahike.query.estimate/estimate-pattern-with-bindings` now sees a sparse id space: measure plan choices on default's slow queries after). For a single-branch store the counter equals that branch's max-eid at every step, so Datahike's own tests see identical eids; only branch tests can differ. Lurking problems checked: an old record without :max-eid is not a head (stored-db?); a roster branch whose head is missing raises :head-commit-missing, a corrupt store either way; history, as-of and since values delegate -max-eid to their origin and cannot be written; imports allocate through the same call site; a deleted branch inside the retention window is not in the roster, so an eid that lived only there can be reissued and a by-commit read of that lineage sees the number mean something else there: accepted, an eid is meaningful only within one db value, nothing joins lineages by eid, and the squash needs uniqueness among entities reachable from the roster's heads, which the seed gives; 2^29 ids for the store's life (default holds about 60k), uncommitted allocations are gaps. The mutable cell is the store's eid allocator, increment-only and rederived from committed heads on open, the class of Datahike's own writer state; record it in the ledger (3.19) by that name. Valid because one process root holds the store's lifetime lock. Cost: about 10 ns per eid, no I/O. Prove: in reference-code/datahike `clojure -M:test -m kaocha.runner :clj-pss` (tests.edn; 952 tests when H2 ran it) green; then the fork bump commits as a platform slice with `--test seon.db-test`, `--test seon.cluster.registry-test`, `--test seon.cluster.source-test`. Proofs: this test (two forks of one commit through `seon.cluster.registry/fork!` each transact a new entity through `seon.db/transact!`: the eids differ and are below tx0); `seon.db-test/an-explicit-eid-outside-the-allocators-range-is-refused`; a-reopened-store-allocates-above-every-heads-max-eid (a fresh store closed and reopened allocates above every head's max-eid); cost row: one allocation under 1 us warm through `seon.profile/measure`. Files: reference-code/datahike (fork bump), test/seon/db_test.clj."
             :must-read #{datahike.db.transaction/next-eid datahike.db.transaction/transact-tx-data datahike.db.transaction/entity-map->op-vec datahike.db.transaction/advance-max-eid datahike.writing/transact! datahike.writing/resolve-head datahike.writing/stored-db? datahike.connector/connect datahike.versioning/branch! datahike.core/with seon.cluster.registry/fork! seon.db/transact!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-agent-world.md"
                         "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/eid-ranges-opus.md"
                         "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/eid-ranges-sol.md"}}
